#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# coordinate_bounds

#% text
# `coordinate_bounds(pts)` gives `[[min, max], ...]` of each coordinate of the points in `pts`; `coordinate_bounds(pts, d)` pads each side by `d`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The points are the innermost lists of `pts`, at any depth.

#% item
# The padding is a number, `scaled(s)` (`s` times the width), or a list with one entry per coordinate, each a number, `scaled(s)` or `[below, above]`.

#% item
# Symbolic coordinates give `min` and `max` terms.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
coordinate_bounds([[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 0]])
#% output
#= [[0, 4], [0, 2]]

#% cell
coordinate_bounds([[0, 1], [1, 2], [4, 0]], scaled(1/4))
#% output
#= [[-1, 5], [-1/2, 5/2]]

#% cell
coordinate_bounds([[3, -3], [-3, 3]], [[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[-4, 5], [-6, 7]]

#% cell
coordinate_bounds([[1, 2], [a, b], [3, 2]])
#% output
#= [[min(1, a), max(3, a)], [min(2, b), max(2, b)]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `coordinate_bounding_box`, `coordinate_bounds_array`, `min`, `max`
